    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Empathy – the adoption of a sensory or affective state that is more appropriate to another’s situation than one’s own – is critical for an animal’s ability to extract danger signals from its social environment. Mice express an evolutionarily conserved antecedent of empathy known as the social transfer of pain, where the presence of a conspecific already experiencing pain causes hyperalgesia in test animals that were not subjected to any pain-inducing stimuli. Neuronal projections from the anterior cingulate cortex (ACC) to the nucleus accumbens (NAc) and from the insular cortex (IC) to the basolateral amygdala (BLA) encode the social transfer of pain in mice. However, the relevant input regions to the ACC remain poorly defined. We have previously found the neuronal activity in the anterior paraventricular nucleus of the thalamus (aPVT) to be necessary for the expression of socially transferred pain (unpublished data). Here, we identify the aPVT as an ACC and IC input region, and we further demonstrate that the aPVT-to-ACC and aPVT-to-IC projections are necessary for the transfer of social pain. Chemogenetic inhibition of aPVT terminals either in the ACC or the IC significantly reduced “observer” mice’s expression of socially transferred pain after observing a familiar “demonstrator” mouse in a chronic pain state induced by spared nerve injury (SNI) surgery. This work expands our understanding of the neural circuitry involved in the bottom-up affective processing of empathy in an experimentally accessible animal model. These findings additionally contribute to hypotheses that can be evaluated in human subjects using noninvasive assays, especially given the conservation of ACC and IC function across mice and humans. Finally, a more sophisticated understanding of neural mechanisms of empathy will drive the development of new therapies for the empathy-related deficits associated with a broad range of neurodevelopmental and psychiatric disorders.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
